  "overview": "Tuscany's countryside runs south from Florence through Chianti, the hills of vineyards and olive groves on the way to Siena, then opens into the Val d'Orcia, the valley of cypress-lined roads that UNESCO listed in 2004 for its Renaissance farm landscape. Hill towns crown the ridges. Siena gathers around its shell-shaped Piazza del Campo, San Gimignano still bristles with medieval towers, and Pienza was rebuilt by Pope Pius II as an ideal Renaissance town and is famed for its pecorino. Montepulciano and Montalcino make two of Italy's great Sangiovese reds, Vino Nobile and Brunello. People come to taste in cellars dug beneath the old towns, roll pici (Siena's hand-rolled pasta) in a farmhouse kitchen, and linger over long lunches looking out across the hills.",
